# Deep Research Report: PLN Indonesia Financing, Fintech and Funding Innovation for Energy Transition

## Executive Summary

PT PLN (Persero), Indonesia's state-owned electricity utility, is at the center of the country's energy transition, requiring an estimated USD 246 billion in power sector investments through 2030 to achieve climate targets. This report examines PLN's innovative financing mechanisms, regulatory frameworks, and emerging fintech solutions enabling the energy transition, based on extensive multi-source research.

---

## 1. Green Bonds and Sustainability-Linked Loans

### 1.1 PLN's Sustainable Financing Framework

PLN has developed a comprehensive **PLN New Sustainable Financing Framework** (published within the last year as of 2025) that enables issuance of green, social, and sustainability-linked loans and bonds. The framework includes:

- **Second Party Opinion (SPO) from DNV**: Independent validation confirming alignment with international standards
- **Eligible categories**: Renewable energy (solar, wind, geothermal, hydro), energy efficiency, grid modernization, and clean transportation
- **Use of proceeds**: Finance and refinance projects contributing to Indonesia's 23% renewable energy target by 2025 and net-zero by 2060

*Source: LinkedIn - PLN New Sustainable Financing Framework (https://www.linkedin.com/posts/gregorius-aditya-kevin-13696b84_pln-new-sustainable-financing-framework-activity-7452751353430331392-4El2)*

### 1.2 Green Loan Issuances (2024)

In 2024, PLN secured significant green financing:

- **IDR 12 trillion (USD 775 million)** in green loans from national financial institutions including four major state-owned banks
- **€1.2 billion (IDR 20.15 trillion)** green financing memorandum with Germany's Kreditanstalt für Wiederaufbau (KfW) for pumped storage hydropower and transmission projects

*Sources:*
- *Indonesia Business Post - PLN secures global funding (https://indonesiabusinesspost.com/2130/Politics/pln-secures-global-funding-forelectrification-renewable-energy-development)*
- *ESG News - Indonesia, Germany Invest €1.2 Billion (https://esgnews.com/indonesia-germany-invest-e1-2-billion-green-funding-for-clean-energy-development/)*

### 1.3 Green Bond Market Context

While PLN itself has not yet issued green bonds (as of 2024-2025), Indonesia's sovereign green bond and sukuk program has raised over USD 7.7 billion since 2018, creating a market infrastructure that PLN can leverage. Corporate green bond issuance in Indonesia reached USD 1.61 billion as of September 2024.

*Source: Bank Indonesia Green Policy Q2 2025 (https://www.bi.go.id/en/iru/presentation/Documents/Republic%20of%20Indonesia%20Presentation%20Book%20-%20Green%20Policy%20Q2%202025.pdf)*

---

## 2. OJK POJK 17/2025: Equity Crowdfunding (ECF) Regulation

### 2.1 Regulatory Framework

On July 11, 2025, Indonesia's Financial Services Authority (OJK) issued **Regulation No. 17 of 2025 on Securities Offering through IT-Based Platforms**, replacing POJK No. 57 of 2020. This represents a significant evolution in Indonesia's equity crowdfunding ecosystem:

**Key Features:**
- **Issuer Limits**: Maximum fundraising of IDR 10 billion (USD 708,560) within a 12-month period per issuer
- **Platform Licensing**: Mandatory registration as Electronic System Operators (PSE) with Ministry of Communications
- **Investor Protection**: Tiered investment limits based on investor sophistication
- **Disclosure Requirements**: Enhanced transparency obligations for use of proceeds

### 2.2 Relevance to Energy Transition

POJK 17/2025 creates new opportunities for community-based renewable energy financing:
- **Community solar projects** can access retail investor capital through licensed ECF platforms
- **Distributed energy resources** can be financed through crowd-equity models
- **Energy efficiency cooperatives** can leverage collective investment mechanisms

*Sources:*
- *Agilegal - Securities crowdfunding obligations (https://www.agilegal.id/publication/articles/31216/securities-crowdfunding-in-indonesia-obligations-for-issuers-platform-operators-and-investors)*
- *LinkedIn - OJK issues POJK 17/2025 (https://www.linkedin.com/posts/wahidhasan001_pojk-nomor-17-tahun-2025-activity-7358881172354924544-qpI-)*

---

## 3. POJK 27/2024 jo 23/2025: Aset Keuangan Digital (AKD) Tokenization

### 3.1 Digital Financial Assets Regulation

**POJK No. 27 of 2024** on Trading of Digital Financial Assets Including Crypto Assets, amended by **POJK No. 23 of 2025**, establishes Indonesia's comprehensive regulatory framework for digital assets:

**Core Provisions:**
- **Asset Classification**: Distinguishes between digital financial assets (DFAs) and crypto assets
- **Tokenization Framework**: Enables tokenization of real-world assets (RWAs) including energy assets
- **Licensing Requirements**: Minimum paid-up capital of IDR 100 billion for digital asset trading platforms
- **OJK Supervision**: Transfer of crypto asset oversight from Bappebti to OJK

### 3.2 Energy Asset Tokenization Potential

The regulation creates pathways for PLN to tokenize energy assets:

**Tokenizable Assets:**
- Renewable energy certificates (RECs)
- Power purchase agreements (PPAs)
- Carbon credits from IDXCarbon
- Energy infrastructure assets (solar farms, wind turbines)
- Grid modernization assets

**Technical Requirements:**
- Blockchain-based ownership records
- Smart contract automation for revenue distribution
- Compliance with ASEAN Digital Taxonomy

*Sources:*
- *OJK Official - POJK 23/2025 Amendment (https://ojk.go.id/id/regulasi/Pages/POJK-23-2025-Perubahan-POJK-27-Tahun-2024-tentang-Penyelenggaraan-Perdagangan-Aset-Keuangan-Digital-Termasuk-Aset-Kripto.aspx)*
- *Veritask - Expansion of Crypto Assets (https://veritask.ai/en/artikel/ekspansi-aset-kripto-dan-produk-derivatif-dalam-pojk-no-23-tahun-2025)*
- *AHP - Indonesia's Push to Regulate Digital Financial Assets (https://www.ahp.id/indonesias-push-to-regulate-digital-financial-asset-offerings-a-framework-for-crypto-and-token-issuers/)*

---

## 4. IDXCarbon Carbon Market

### 4.1 Market Structure

**IDXCarbon**, operated by Indonesia Stock Exchange (IDX), is Indonesia's official carbon trading platform launched in September 2023:

**Market Performance (2024):**
- **Volume**: 413,764 tons traded in 2024
- **Average Price**: IDR 55,985 (USD 3.9) per ton CO2e in December 2024
- **Participants**: Initially limited to PLN-connected coal-fired power plants for compliance phase

### 4.2 PLN's Role

PLN plays a dual role in IDXCarbon:
1. **Compliance Obligor**: Coal-fired power plants under PLN grid must surrender carbon allowances
2. **REC Seller**: PLN Indonesia Power offers carbon products from renewable energy plants

**Trading Phases:**
- **Phase 1 (2023-2024)**: PLN-connected CFPPs only
- **Phase 2 (2025+)**: Expansion to other sectors and international trading

### 4.3 Integration with Energy Transition

IDXCarbon revenues can fund:
- Coal retirement mechanisms
- Renewable energy deployment
- Energy efficiency improvements
- Just transition programs for affected workers

*Sources:*
- *IDXCarbon Official Website (https://idxcarbon.co.id/)*
- *IEEFA - Two Years After Launch (https://ieefa.org/resources/two-years-after-launch-indonesias-carbon-market-struggles-find-momentum)*
- *ICAP - Indonesian Economic Value of Carbon (https://icapcarbonaction.com/en/ets/indonesian-economic-value-carbon-nilai-ekonomi-karbon-trading-scheme)*

---

## 5. RWA Blockchain for Energy Assets

### 5.1 Blockchain-Based REC System

Research published in 2025 demonstrates blockchain's potential for Indonesia's renewable energy certificate (REC) market:

**Technical Architecture:**
- **Tokenization**: Convert RECs into digital tokens on blockchain
- **APX-TIGR Validation**: PLN represents renewable energy plants; APX-TIGR validates and marketplace for transactions
- **Smart Contracts**: Automate REC issuance, transfer, and retirement

**Benefits:**
- Enhanced security and traceability
- Reduced transaction costs
- Real-time verification
- Improved stakeholder trust

### 5.2 Peer-to-Peer Energy Trading

PLN has explored blockchain-based P2P energy trading:
- **Pilot Projects**: Joint initiatives with technology partners like Chaintope
- **Gumelar District Model**: Blockchain-enabled P2P trading for community microgrids
- **Virtual Power Plants**: Blockchain coordination of distributed energy resources

### 5.3 PLN's Blockchain Initiatives

- **Renewable Energy Certificate Users**: Increased 117% in 2024, with ~2,000 corporate customers
- **REC Issuance**: ~8 TWh issued since 2021, projected to reach 7.4 TWh/year by 2030

*Sources:*
- *PMC - Blockchain-based REC system (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11847093/)*
- *MDPI - Stakeholders' Perceptions of P2P Energy Trading (https://www.mdpi.com/1996-1073/17/19/4956)*
- *ResearchGate - Blockchain-Based REC System (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/388488881_Blockchain-Based_Renewable_Energy_Certificate_System_in_Indonesia)*
- *Tanah Air - PLN REC Users Increase 117% (https://tanahair.net/pln-renewable-energy-certificate-users-increase-117-in-2024/)*

---

## 6. Blended Finance Mechanisms

### 6.1 Energy Transition Mechanism (ETM)

Indonesia's **Energy Transition Mechanism** operates as a blended finance platform:

**Structure:**
- **Concessional Finance**: Low-cost loans from multilateral development banks
- **Commercial Finance**: Market-rate debt from commercial banks
- **Grant Components**: Technical assistance and capacity building
- **Risk Mitigation**: Guarantees and first-loss provisions

**PLN's Role:**
- **Cirebon Electric Power**: ADB signed MoU for coal retirement
- **Blended Finance Platform**: Established under Ministry of Finance authority

### 6.2 Key Blended Finance Transactions

1. **ADB Results-Based Loan**: USD 470 million to PLN for clean energy transition
2. **KfW-EIB Joint Funding**: €1.2 billion for pumped storage hydropower
3. **ADB-AIIB Solar Funding**: USD 37.2 million for Hijaunesia Program GCL Solar Subprojects

### 6.3 Domestic Blended Finance

- **PLN Green Loan**: IDR 12 trillion from national financial institutions (2024)
- **Climate Investment Fund-Accelerated Coal Transition (CIF-ACT)**: Supporting coal transition
- **Green Finance Facility**: Technical assistance from PPIAF for PLN

*Sources:*
- *ADB - Boosts Indonesia's Clean Energy Transition (https://www.adb.org/news/adb-boosts-indonesias-clean-energy-transition)*
- *ScienceDirect - Competing Energy Transition Rationales (https://www.sciencedirect.com/article/pii/S2214629624003888)*
- *Climate Policy Initiative - Optimizing Public Finance (https://www.climatepolicyinitiative.org/publication/energizing-renewables-in-indonesia-optimizing-public-finance-levers-to-drive-private-investment/)*

---

## 7. JETP/CIFF Funding

### 7.1 Just Energy Transition Partnership (JETP)

**Indonesia JETP** is a USD 20 billion commitment from International Partners Group (IPG):

**Funding Structure:**
- **Grants**: USD 153.8 million (7.69% of total)
- **Concessional Loans**: Low-cost financing from donor countries
- **Commercial Loans**: Market-rate financing
- **Private Investment**: Mobilized through de-risking mechanisms

**PLN's Role:**
- **Primary Offtaker**: Long-term PPAs with independent power producers (IPPs)
- **Coal Phase-out**: Target to retire 1 GW of coal capacity by 2030
- **Renewable Integration**: 500 MW solar projects funded through JETP

### 7.2 CIFF (Children's Investment Fund Foundation) Support

CIFF provides technical assistance and catalytic funding:
- **Policy Support**: Comprehensive Investment and Policy Plan (CIPP) development
- **Coal Transition**: Early retirement mechanisms for PLN coal assets
- **Grid Integration**: Smart grid and energy storage investments

### 7.3 JETP Project Pipeline

- **PLN Indonesia Power**: 500 MW solar in five locations (2024)
- **PLN IP-Acwa Power**: 60 MW solar project (USD 60 million JETP funding, 2025)
- **ADB Program**: 1,800 MW utility-scale solar and wind (mobilizing USD 1 billion private investment)

*Sources:*
- *UNDP - Indonesia JETP (https://www.undp.org/indonesia/projects/indonesia-just-energy-transition-partnership-jetp)*
- *Climate Policy Initiative - JETP Highlights (https://www.climatepolicyinitiative.org/highlights-from-indonesias-jetp-comprehensive-investment-and-policy-plan/)*
- *Argus Media - Indonesia secures $60mn JETP funding (https://www.argusmedia.com/zh/news-and-insights/latest-market-news/2683345-indonesia-secures-60mn-jetp-funding-for-solar-power)*
- *Tanah Air - PLN Indonesia Power JETP 500 MW (https://tanahair.net/pln-indonesia-power-will-utilise-jetp-to-fund-500-mw-solar-power-projects-in-five-locations/)*

---

## 8. Community Crowd-Equity Models

### 8.1 Community-Based Renewable Energy

PLN has implemented community-based models in Eastern Indonesia:

**Projects:**
- **110 kWp Solar Grid (2020)**: Improved electricity access for households
- **MENTARI Program**: UK-funded AC-coupled solar units (60 kWp + 35 kWp)
- **Special Allocation Fund**: IDR 3 billion for communal energy development

### 8.2 Crowdfunding Potential

Under POJK 17/2025, community energy projects can access:
- **Retail Investor Capital**: Through licensed ECF platforms
- **Micro-Investments**: Fractional ownership of renewable energy assets
- **Cooperative Models**: Community-owned solar and micro-hydro projects

### 8.3 PLN's Community Engagement

- **PLN Nusantara Renewables**: Subsidiary focused on community-scale projects
- **Last-Mile Electrification**: Partnerships with UNOPS for 2 GW solar potential
- **Green Energy Buyers Dialogue**: ~2,000 corporate REC customers

*Sources:*
- *Climate Policy Initiative - Community-based RE for Eastern Indonesia (https://www.climatepolicyinitiative.org/electrifying-the-last-mile-community-based-renewable-energy-for-eastern-indonesia/)*
- *SEADS - Electrifying Last Mile (https://seads.adb.org/articles/electrifying-last-mile-community-based-renewable-energy-eastern-indonesia)*
- *PLN Nusantara Renewables (https://ptplnnr.com/)*

